{"t":1790891413,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"9b0aff0d-26f7-4cc3-90df-cae241fe42d3","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-01T20:56:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 012056 CCA\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n456 PM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes were made to the forecast at this time. \n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) A stalling cold front may bring rain at times from Friday \n  through Sunday.\n\n- 2) First widespread frost and freeze potential west of the\n  Blue Ridge early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalling cold front may bring rain at times \nfrom Friday through Sunday.\n\nA cold front will approach from the northwest late Friday \nafternoon into Friday evening before eventually pushing south \nof the area Friday night. Showers will likely reach western Maryland \nby the afternoon, progressing southeastward during the evening and \ninto the night. Despite the warm temperatures, poor lapse rates \naloft will limit instability, so few if any thunderstorms are \nexpected. A westerly component to the flow could also result in \ndownslope drying, and many models show showers becoming more \nscattered in nature with eastward extent. Rainfall amounts with this \nround will likely be less than a quarter inch for most locations.\n\nStrong high pressure will pass north of the area Saturday while\nthe front stalls across the southeastern states. Southern stream\nmoisture will likely overrun the boundary fairly far to the\nnorth, but there remains some uncertainty in the dividing line\nbetween rain and no rain. Overall, Saturday probably has a\nbetter chance of being dry, especially the further north you go \nwith strong high pressure set up to the north. \n\nA wave of low pressure will likely ride along the boundary \nSaturday night into Sunday, and this may provide a higher chance\nof rain spreading northward into the area. Latest guidance has \nmaintained a northward shift with rainfall coverage during this \nperiod. In the end, the details are far from solidified. However, \nany rain that does occur will likely exit the area Sunday evening.\n\nBeing north of the boundary and potentially having a lot of\ncloud cover, high temperatures will likely remain in the 60s for\nmost areas over the weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... First widespread frost and freeze potential west of \nthe Blue Ridge early next week.\n\nThe true Fall feel sets in next week with highs and lows near to \nbelow normal. Monday will feature broad 1025-1028 mb high pressure \nbuilding east from the Upper Midwest/western Great Lakes behind a \ndeparting upper trof/secondary cold front exiting the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast. The frontal passage itself appears mostly dry with perhaps a \nlingering shower/sprinkle if any moisture is leftover. Breezy west \nto northwest winds will accompany the front with gusts 20-30 mph and \nhigh temperatures in the upper 60s to mid 70s (upper 50s mountains).\n\nHigh pressure moves overhead Monday night into Tuesday allowing the \nwinds to lighten and skies to clear. This will yield a prime \nradiational cooling setup, especially west of the Blue Ridge where \nwidespread frost and freeze conditions are possible. Of particular \nconcern is the high elevations and sheltered valleys (cold sinks) \nwhich could drop down to around freezing. Elsewhere, expect lows in \nthe upper 30s to mid 40s. Locations near the water will be in the \nlow 50s. Similar conditions are expected Tuesday night with \nwidespread mid to upper 30s west of the Blue Ridge and north/west of \nthe I-95 metro areas. These chilly conditions (concern for frost) \nare roughly 1 to 2 weeks earlier than average.\n\nOutside of the frost and freeze potential, dry conditions will \nprevail for much of the week with broad high pressure overhead. A \ncold front looks to cross the region Thursday into Friday, perhaps \nbringing a few showers to the mountains. Otherwise, another large \narea of high pressure returns for the weekend with warming \ntemperatures heading into the following workweek. Highs Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday will likely be in the 60s (50s mountains), with \nlows in the 40s to near 50 (30s mountains). Thursday and Friday, \nhighs warm back into the upper 60s to mid 70s (low 60s mountains) \nwith lows in the 40s and 50s.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected through midday Friday with light \nsoutherly winds.\n\nA cold front will enter the area late Friday afternoon or\nevening. Some showers are possible along the front, but they\nwill likely be fairly light. A brief period of MVFR conditions\nis possible Friday night.\n\nThe front will stall to the south over the weekend, but its\nposition remains uncertain. Saturday has the greater chance of\nbeing dry, with a wave of low pressure potentially bringing rain\non Sunday. Any sub-VFR conditions would likely be limited to CHO\nSaturday, but could spread northward Sunday.\n\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail Monday through Thursday, \nalthough river valley fog is possible each morning. Winds will be \nout of the north and northwest Monday with gusts around 20 to 25 \nkts. North to northwesterly winds continue Tuesday before shifting \nto the south Wednesday into Thursday. Speeds drop below 15 kts \nduring this time.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSoutherly winds will uptick a little today into Friday. A Small \nCraft Advisory may be needed on the wider waters of the middle \nChesapeake Bay tonight into Friday morning, but confidence is lower \nsince a southwesterly component to the flow would limit channeling \neffects. A cold front will push southward Friday night, with \nadvisories possible in the ensuing northerly flow through parts of \nSaturday. After that, winds may be relatively lighter until a second \ncold front pushes through on Monday.\n\nHigh end SCA conditions are possible Monday into Tuesday in the wake \nof a departing cold frontal boundary. Northerly winds will gust 25 \nto 30 kts before dropping off Tuesday morning. A few gusts near gale \nforce are possible if the stronger solutions verify. Sub-SCA \nconditions are expected under high pressure Tuesday afternoon \nthrough Thursday. Winds will switch from the north back to the south \nby the middle of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nSome uncertainty continues to exist through Friday as tide \nlevels remain elevated and subtly fluctuating. These small \nchanges will make the difference in whether there is additional \nminor flooding. Alexandria, Dahlgren, Annapolis, and Havre de Grace \nhave the greatest chance of reaching flood stage. An advisory has \nbeen issued for Annapolis where confidence is highest. The evening \nhigh tides are favored for any flooding since they are \nastronomically higher. A cold frontal passage should lead to \ndeclining water levels Friday night.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/CJL/EST\nAVIATION...CJL/EST\nMARINE...CJL/EST\n"},"forecast":{"t":1790891365,"days":{"2026-10-01":{"hi":84,"lo":65,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-02":{"hi":89,"lo":61,"pop":49,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-10-03":{"hi":68,"lo":58,"pop":50,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-10-04":{"hi":66,"lo":58,"pop":74,"short":"Showers And Thunderstorms Likely"},"2026-10-05":{"hi":70,"lo":49,"pop":3,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-06":{"hi":61,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-07":{"hi":64,"lo":50,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1790888400,"n":48,"ceil":[25000,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,24900,null,null,null,24900,24900,11000,11000,6900,9300,8900,7400,7000,6400,6800,8000,8700,9200,9600,9200,8900,8600,8100,8100,8100,7600,7600,7600,8800,8800